      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the relationship between pancreatic enzyme therapy (PET) and the clinical outcomes of growth, abdominal pain, constipation, gassiness, and number of stools in cystic fibrosis (CF)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Patients (n = 1215) >4 weeks of age from 33 Cystic Fibrosis Foundation accredited sites who had a sweat chloride >60 mmol/L or two CF-causing mutations were enrolled using a proportionate sampling strategy in a nonblinded study. Patients submitted a stool sample and completed a questionnaire. The study coordinator also completed a questionnaire for each patient. Enzyme dosing and growth, abdominal pain, gassiness, constipation, and number of stools were compared."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the 1215 enrolled patients, 1131 (93.1%) were prescribed PET. Only 14.9% had pancreatic function assessed before enrolling in this study. Stool elastase-1 analysis identified 1074 (89%) patients as pancreatic insufficient (PI). There was no association between PET and the outcomes: growth, abdominal pain, gassiness, constipation, and number of stools."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PET dose is not correlated with growth or gastrointestinal symptoms. More sensitive outcome measures of the effectiveness of PET in patients with CF are needed to guide treatment of PI."
        }
      ]},
